    description: "When your AHJ requires sealed engineering, a GC's permit stalls without a PE stamp. How review-and-seal keeps submittals moving in AZ, CA, TX, NV, and FL.",
    h1: "Do General Contractors Need a PE Stamp for Permits?",
    answer: "Yes — whenever the authority having jurisdiction requires engineering on the permit set, the general contractor's submittal needs a PE stamp, and that seal exists only after a licensed engineer has done a real review. For GCs, the trigger is usually one of three things: the project type or size crosses the state threshold for sealed engineering (Florida's thresholds are the strictest and most explicit), the drawings include delegated structural or MEP design that the building department wants sealed, or plan check comes back with corrections that require engineering revision and re-seal. The seal is not a formality you buy at the end — it is the engineer's professional statement that the design was reviewed under responsible charge, the calculations were verified, and the revisions the review required were made.\n\nI work with GCs who get caught at permit intake because the engineering scope was never nailed down during bidding. The most expensive version of this is the GC who priced the job assuming the architect's set was permit-ready, only to have the city require a sealed structural package or sealed MEP design the architect never provided. In Texas, TBPELS Rule 137.33 means the sealing engineer must take responsible charge — the design has to be reviewed by or under the direct supervision of the Texas-licensed PE who seals it — so a last-minute \"find me a stamp\" call does not work the way contractors wish it did. The engagement I run for GCs is review-and-seal: I review the design, verify or perform the calculations, mark every required revision, review the corrections, and then seal.\n\nThe practical move is to engage the reviewing PE during preconstruction or early in the submittal cycle, not after the first rejection. In Arizona the Board of Technical Registration, in Nevada the state board, in California the professional engineers act, and in Florida the Board of Professional Engineers all expect the same thing: a seal backed by genuine review. When the PE is in early, corrections are design fixes the engineer handles in days; when the PE arrives after a rejection, corrections are schedule damage. Your permit submittal moves at the speed of its sealed engineering — get the review started before the clock starts.",
