    h1: "How Does PE Stamping Work on GC-Led Design-Build Projects?",
    answer: "On GC-led design-build, PE stamping works the same legal way it works everywhere — a licensed engineer takes responsible charge of a real review, verifies the calculations, requires revisions, and then seals — but the coordination is harder because design is still moving while the GC is already pricing, permitting, and mobilizing. The GC holds both the design contract and the construction contract, which means the GC also owns the engineering accountability: the engineer of record (EOR) has to be engaged early enough to review design as it develops, not handed a \"finished\" set the week before permit intake. In Texas, TBPELS Rule 137.33 is explicit that sealing follows responsible charge, and responsible charge cannot be manufactured after the fact.\n\nI see GC-led design-build teams make the same structural mistake: they treat the EOR as a submittal vendor instead of a design partner. The EOR gets brought in to \"seal the set\" after the design-build architect and the MEP design-assist subs have already locked the scheme — and then the PE's review finds load-path problems or undersized systems that require redesign while steel is on order. The correct sequence is the opposite: the EOR reviews the structural and MEP concepts at schematic and design-development stages, verifies the calculations behind the pricing set, and stays in responsible charge through permit drawings. The seal at the end is then a non-event, because the review happened all along.\n\nFor GCs running design-build in Arizona, California, Texas, Nevada, or Florida, the practical question is always schedule overlap: how do you keep design moving fast without outrunning the engineering review? The answer is staged review — the PE reviews and seals packages in the order the AHJ accepts them (foundations, then structure, then MEP), so early work permits while later design is still being reviewed. That only works if the EOR is contracted from day one with a review scope that matches the fast-track sequence. Price the EOR as core team, not as a permit expense, and the submittal stops being the bottleneck.",
