    title: "Who Handles Plan-Check Corrections That Need a PE Re-Seal?",
    description: "Plan-check corrections requiring engineering revision need a PE's review and re-seal. How GCs get corrections answered fast without stalling the permit.",
    h1: "Who Handles Plan-Check Corrections That Need a PE Re-Seal?",
    answer: "Plan-check corrections that require engineering revision are handled by the licensed PE in responsible charge of that discipline — the same engineer who sealed the original submittal — because only that engineer can revise the design, re-verify the calculations, and re-seal the corrected sheets. For the GC, this is where permits are won or lost: corrections split into two piles, the clarifications the GC or architect can answer directly (a missing detail reference, a code section citation) and the engineering corrections that need the PE's revised analysis and a fresh seal. Mis-sorting those piles is the classic delay — the GC spends two weeks drafting a response to a structural correction the PE needed to redesign, or waits on the PE for a clarification anyone could have answered.\n\nI triage corrections the day they arrive. Engineering corrections — anything touching loads, member sizes, system capacities, equipment selections, or code compliance analysis — go straight to the sealing PE with the reviewer's comment, the relevant sheets, and a deadline tied to the resubmittal date. The PE revises the design, re-verifies the calculations, clouds the changes, and re-seals the affected sheets; the re-seal is never a signature over unreviewed changes but a fresh professional statement covering the revised design. Non-engineering corrections get answered in parallel so the resubmittal package is complete. The discipline that matters most is completeness: AHJs in Phoenix, Las Vegas, Houston, Miami, and Los Angeles all penalize piecemeal resubmittals, and a resubmittal missing one corrected sheet can restart the review cycle.\n\nThe GC's leverage here is entirely about who is already engaged. If the sealing PE is under contract with a defined corrections scope, engineering corrections turn around in days and the re-seal is part of the engagement. If the original engineer is unresponsive, out of business, or was never properly engaged, the GC is starting a new review-and-seal engagement mid-crisis — the new PE must review the whole design under responsible charge before sealing anything, which takes weeks, not days. I write a corrections allowance into every GC engagement for exactly this reason: plan check always has comments, and the permit schedule should assume at least one full correction round with engineering revision.",
