    h1: "Can an Out-of-State GC Get a PE Stamp in Arizona, California, Texas, Nevada, or Florida?",
    answer: "Yes, but the PE seal on your permit set must come from an engineer licensed in the state where you are building — your GC license, your home-state relationships, and your design team's credentials do not substitute for it. Every one of the five states requires the sealing PE to hold its own license: Arizona through the Board of Technical Registration, California through its professional engineers act, Texas through TBPELS (plus a firm F-number for the engineering company), Nevada through its state board, and Florida through the Board of Professional Engineers. An out-of-state PE can pursue comity licensure, but comity takes weeks to months — it is a licensing project, not a permit strategy.\n\nI work with GCs expanding into new states who discover this at the worst moment: the design is done, the owner wants permits next month, and the engineer who designed everything is licensed in the wrong state. The design itself is usually fine — engineering is engineering — but the seal has to come from a PE who takes responsible charge under the project state's board rules, which means a licensed in-state engineer must review the design, verify the calculations, require revisions, and then seal. That is a real review-and-seal engagement, and it takes real calendar time. The GCs who plan for it engage the in-state PE during design; the ones who do not end up redesigning the schedule around the review.\n\nEach state also has its own flavor beyond the license. Texas enforces responsible charge through Rule 137.33 and requires the firm's F-number on sealed documents. California layers Title 24 energy compliance and seismic detailing expectations that an out-of-state engineer may never have designed to. Florida applies explicit statutory thresholds for when sealed engineering is required and enforces wind provisions aggressively. Arizona and Nevada have their own board rules on sealing and responsible charge. The in-state PE does more than provide a lawful seal — they translate the design into what the local AHJ actually expects to see.",
    directAnswer: "An out-of-state GC can build in AZ, CA, TX, NV, or FL, but the permit set must be sealed by a PE licensed in that state after a genuine review under responsible charge. Engage the in-state reviewing PE during design — comity licensure takes too long to be a permit strategy.",
