    h1: "How Do Architects Engage an Engineer of Record for Review-and-Seal?",
    answer: "Architects engage an engineer of record for review-and-seal by contracting a licensed PE to take responsible charge of the engineering scope: the EOR reviews the design, verifies or performs the calculations, requires revisions, and seals the permit set. The engagement usually starts one of two ways — the architect brings the EOR onto the design team from the beginning as the structural or MEP designer, or the architect (or the owner, or the GC) brings the EOR in later to review and seal a design that someone else produced. Both are legitimate; what is not legitimate is a seal without the review. In Texas that line is drawn explicitly by TBPELS Rule 137.33, and every other state board draws it in its own words: the PE seals only work performed under their responsible charge.\n\nI advise architects to define the EOR's scope in writing at engagement, because \"review and seal\" means different things to different people. The scope I work to includes the design phases covered (schematic review through permit), the disciplines (structural, mechanical, electrical, plumbing, or a subset), the deliverables (calculations, sealed drawings, energy documentation, responses to plan-check corrections), and the revision protocol — who revises, who re-reviews, and how many correction rounds are included. Architects who leave this vague discover mid-project that the EOR's fee covered one review pass while the project needed three, and the resulting argument about extra services poisons the team.\n\nTiming matters as much as scope. An EOR engaged at schematic design shapes the structural system and the MEP concept while changes are cheap; an EOR engaged at 100% CDs performs an audit of decisions already locked in, and the review findings become expensive rework. I tell architects the EOR is cheapest at the beginning and most expensive at the end — not because the fee changes much, but because the cost of acting on the review findings does. Engage early, define the scope in writing, and the seal at permit time is the quiet conclusion of a review that ran all along.",
    directAnswer: "Architects engage an EOR by contracting a licensed PE to take responsible charge of the engineering scope — reviewing the design, verifying calculations, requiring revisions, and sealing the set. Define the scope in writing at engagement and bring the EOR in early, when review findings are still cheap to act on.",
