        body: "The first model is the EOR as design partner: the architect engages the structural or MEP engineer at project inception, the engineer designs the systems through the phases, and the seal at permit time covers work the PE authored. This is the smoothest path — responsible charge is natural because the PE did the work, coordination is continuous, and there are no surprises at the end. Most established architect-engineer teams work this way, and the fees reflect a full design scope.\n\nThe second model is the EOR as reviewing engineer: the design exists — produced by the architect's staff, a design-build entity, or a previous engineer — and the PE is engaged to review it, verify the calculations, require revisions, and seal. This is the true review-and-seal engagement, and it is where the discipline matters most: the PE must actually review, not just read. I take these engagements seriously and price them for the review effort they require, because a reviewing EOR who finds real problems is doing exactly what the owner paid for. Architects should expect the review to produce revision comments — a review that finds nothing on a complex set was not a review.",
