    title: "How Do Architects Get MEP Designs Reviewed and Sealed?",
    description: "Architects coordinating MEP consultants need sealed mechanical, electrical, and plumbing designs. What the MEP review covers before the PE seals.",
    h1: "How Do Architects Get MEP Designs Reviewed and Sealed?",
    answer: "Architects get MEP designs reviewed and sealed by engaging a licensed MEP engineer — either as the project's MEP engineer of record from the start, or as a reviewing PE when the MEP design comes from a design-build subcontractor or another source. The PE takes responsible charge of the mechanical, electrical, and plumbing scope: reviewing the system designs, verifying the load calculations and equipment selections, checking code compliance across all three disciplines, requiring revisions, and sealing the MEP permit set. For the architect, the MEP seal is often the long pole in the permit tent — MEP reviewers generate the most correction comments in most jurisdictions, and an unreviewed MEP set is where permits go to stall.\n\nI tell architects the MEP review lives or dies on coordination inputs. The MEP engineer needs the architectural backgrounds — floor plans, reflected ceiling plans, building sections, the space program — in a form that shows where systems actually have to fit. The classic architect-MEP failure is ceiling space: the design shows 12-foot ceilings, the structural beams take 24 inches, the ductwork needs 18, the lighting and sprinkler need their clearances, and nobody did the arithmetic until the MEP PE's review flagged it. An MEP review that starts with complete architectural backgrounds finds these conflicts on paper; one that starts with partial backgrounds finds them in the field.\n\nThe review itself covers the full MEP scope: HVAC load calculations verified against realistic design conditions, equipment selections matched to the loads (including latent capacity in humid climates like Houston, Miami, and Tampa), plumbing riser logic and fixture counts, electrical distribution from service to branch circuits with coordinated protection, lighting and controls, and energy compliance documentation reconciled to the actual design — Title 24 in California, the applicable energy code elsewhere. When the loads are verified, the systems coordinate, and the energy forms match the drawings, the PE seals and the MEP set goes to permit as a package that survives plan check.",
