    description: "The architect of record owns the building design; the engineer of record owns the engineering. How the two roles divide responsibility on a permitted project.",
    h1: "What Is the Difference Between the Architect of Record and the Engineer of Record?",
    answer: "The architect of record is the licensed architect responsible for the building's overall design — the architecture, the code analysis, the coordination of the drawing set — and seals the architectural sheets. The engineer of record is the licensed PE responsible for the engineering systems — structural, mechanical, electrical, plumbing, civil — who takes responsible charge of the engineering design, verifies the calculations, and seals the engineering sheets. They are parallel professionals with parallel seals: the AHJ expects the architectural set sealed by the architect of record and the engineering sheets sealed by the EOR, each certifying their own scope.\n\nI explain the division this way: the architect of record decides what the building is, and the engineer of record proves it stands up, stays comfortable, and operates safely. The architect owns the program, the aesthetics, the life-safety code analysis, and the coordination of all the disciplines' work into one coherent set. The EOR owns the engineering decisions inside that set — the structural system and its calculations, the HVAC loads and equipment selections, the electrical distribution, the plumbing design — and the professional liability that comes with sealing them. Neither can do the other's job: the architect cannot seal engineering beyond their licensed scope, and the engineer does not seal architecture.\n\nWhere projects get confused is the overlap zone — coordination. The architect of record coordinates the drawing set, which means chasing the EOR's backgrounds, reconciling the structural grid with the program, and making sure the MEP routing the EOR designed actually fits the architecture. But coordination is not responsible charge: the architect coordinating the set does not become responsible for the engineering, and the EOR reviewing backgrounds does not become responsible for the architecture. I document the boundary in the EOR agreement — disciplines, deliverables, and where the EOR's scope stops — so that when plan check asks a structural question, everyone knows whose seal answers it.",
