    slug: "developer-pe-stamp-due-diligence",
    title: "What Engineering Due Diligence Does a Developer Need Before Closing on a Site?",
    description: "Before closing, developers need engineering due diligence: feasibility, entitlements risk, and permit-path review. What a PE checks before you commit capital.",
    h1: "What Engineering Due Diligence Does a Developer Need Before Closing on a Site?",
    answer: "Before closing, a developer needs engineering due diligence that answers three questions: can the intended project physically work on this site, what will the jurisdiction require before it permits, and what will the engineering cost and cost in time. I perform this as a PE-level feasibility review — reading the geotechnical data (or flagging its absence), checking floodplain and drainage conditions, reviewing the site's utility capacity and access, testing the program against zoning and setback constraints from an engineering perspective, and mapping the permit path: which AHJ, what sealed engineering the submittal will require, and where the entitlement risks sit. The deliverable is a written findings memo the developer can underwrite against, not a stamp — no PE seals a feasibility memo, because there is no design yet to take responsible charge of.\n\nI have watched developers skip this and pay for it in the seven figures. The pattern is always the same: the site looked clean on the broker's flyer, and then the geotechnical report showed expansive soils requiring deep foundations, or the floodplain map put half the site in a regulatory floodway, or the sewer district had no capacity and the off-site upgrade cost more than the land. A few weeks of engineering due diligence before close — or structured as a contingency during the feasibility period — surfaces these as priced risks while the developer still has leverage: renegotiate, reprogram, or walk away.\n\nThe due diligence also sets up the review-and-seal work that follows. When I do feasibility for a developer, I am already learning the site's constraints, the AHJ's expectations, and the program's engineering drivers — so when the project moves into design, the EOR engagement starts with context instead of from zero. Developers building a pipeline in Arizona, Texas, or Florida get compounding value here: each site's due diligence sharpens the next one's, and the PE relationship carries across acquisitions. Spend the diligence money before close; it is the cheapest capital on the project.",
    directAnswer: "Developers need PE-level engineering due diligence before closing: a feasibility review of soils, floodplain, utilities, and zoning constraints, plus a permit-path map showing which AHJ, what sealed engineering, and where the entitlement risks sit — delivered as a written findings memo to underwrite against.",
