        body: "I work through the site systematically. Soils and foundations first: the existing geotechnical data gets read for bearing conditions, expansive-soil risk, groundwater, and what foundation systems the site will support — and if there is no geotechnical data, that is itself a finding, priced as the cost of the investigation plus the foundation contingency the unknown requires. Floodplain and drainage next: the FEMA maps, the local drainage criteria, and whether the site's grading concept can actually move water the way the jurisdiction requires. Then utilities — water, sewer, power, gas — checked for capacity and proximity, because off-site extensions are the budget item developers most consistently underestimate.\n\nZoning and entitlements get an engineering read: setbacks, height limits, density, parking ratios, and landscape requirements tested against the program massing to confirm the intended project fits the entitlements envelope — or to quantify exactly what variance or rezoning risk the program carries. Finally the permit path: which AHJ has jurisdiction, what their submittal requirements and review timelines look like, what sealed engineering the project will need, and where the discretionary approvals sit. The memo ties it together as priced risks with recommended next steps, written for the investment committee, not for engineers.",
